WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
She keeps the score from her third attempt.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Meanwhile, Vera Navalikhina remains in first place
with the score from her opening run.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
And here comes her fourth attempt today.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Vera opens with remarkable consistency.
A strong, high-amplitude run.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Steady.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:39.000
And a huge disaster—
the exit and slide into a lipslide.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Very impressive. She is building even more speed
for the final trick.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Wow! A beautiful air over the corner.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
She still has speed and time.
What does she have planned?

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
That ride sets her up for one more trick.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
This is a flawless run, everyone.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Look at her—not a muscle in her face moved.
She is completely composed.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Although it is clear: this is a winning run.
She shakes her head as if she cannot believe it.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Usually we see skaters land a trick in warm-up,
then struggle to bring it into their run.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Look—her mother is crying.
This time it is the other way around.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Incredible emotion. She could not land this trick
during warm-up.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
But here, in the final, she rode away.
Bravo—fantastic.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Let us watch the replay.
Everything looks flawless and clean.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.000
That disaster was huge.
Absolutely incredible.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Then the corner air—an indy—
and a backside 360 with a melon grab.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Outstanding. Well done.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:26.000
“Melon” is short for “melancholy.”

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:36.000
The front hand grabs the heel edge behind the front foot,
with a slight extension. A beautiful melon.
